# Line endings are normalised here rather than left to each machine's git
# configuration, because getting them wrong is not a cosmetic problem.
#
# A shell script checked out with CRLF fails in a way that reads as nonsense:
#
#   $'\r': command not found
#   set: pipefail: invalid option name
#
# bash treats the carriage return as part of the command name. That cost a
# smoke run: run_suite.sh had been committed with CRLF from a Windows editor,
# and the Codespace faithfully executed a script whose every line ended in an
# invisible character.
#
# `* text=auto` tells git to normalise line endings to LF in the repository
# for anything it detects as text. The explicit rules below remove the
# guesswork for the files where being wrong actually breaks something.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Executed by a shell. CRLF here is a hard failure, never a nuisance.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Makefile    text eol=lf

# Consumed by Linux tooling inside containers.
Dockerfile  text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# Source and config. LF everywhere keeps diffs meaningful across machines.
*.py        text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.json      text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf
.env.example text eol=lf

# Binary: never touch these. Normalising a gzip would corrupt it, and the
# dataset is checksum-verified, so corruption would surface as a confusing
# integrity failure rather than as a line-ending mistake.
*.gz        binary
*.png       binary
*.pdf       binary
